= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

<Hydrostatic pressure> varies with height as $p_l-p_{\rm atm}=-\rho gz$ when the far flat bath is the zero-height reference. <Surface tension> balances that pressure jump by <curvature>; with the <curvature> sign chosen in the equation this becomes $\rho gz=\sigma\kappa$. Here $\rho$ is liquid <mass density>, $g$ <gravitational acceleration>, $\sigma$ <surface tension> and $\kappa$ the signed planar <curvature>. The <capillary length> $\ell_c=\sqrt{\sigma/(\rho g)}$ balances these effects. Scale both physical $z$ and $h$ by $\ell_c$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{z=h_{zz}(1+h_z^2)^{-3/2}.}
$$
For the wall-rising <meniscus> $h$ decreases as $z$ rises. At the distant bath, $z\downarrow0$, $h\to\infty$ and $h_z\to-\infty$, because the free surface becomes horizontal when expressed as $z(x)$. In the first integral this gives $\boxed{A=-1}$.

At zero wall <contact angle> the interface is tangent to the vertical wall. In the representation $x=h(z)$ this means $h(z_0)=0$ and $h_z(z_0)=0$, so $z_0^2/2-1=0$. The rising contact point is $\boxed{z_0=\sqrt2}$. Put $q=z^2/2-1$. Then $h_z=q/\sqrt{1-q^2}=\sqrt2(z-z_0)+O((z-z_0)^2)$. Integration from the contact point gives
$$
\boxed{h(z)\sim\frac{(z-z_0)^2}{\sqrt2}.}
$$
The sign of $h_z$ is negative on the approach from below, while $h$ is positive, as required by the geometry. This local quadratic shape is the <zero-contact-angle wall meniscus> matching law.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $z-z_0=\epsilon\bar z$, with physical transverse thickness $h=\delta H$. Matching the preceding quadratic <meniscus> gives $\delta=\epsilon^2$ when the inner amplitude is normalized as $H\sim\bar z^2/\sqrt2$. After multiplying the transformed film equation by $\epsilon$, its two capillary/gravity coefficients are $\delta^3\widehat C\epsilon^{-3}$ and $\delta^2\widehat C$. Setting the <curvature> coefficient to one therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\epsilon=\widehat C^{-1/3},\qquad\delta=\widehat C^{-2/3},\qquad\delta^2\widehat C=\epsilon.}
$$
Thus the inner equation is exactly the stated $\epsilon H_t+v_0H_{\bar z}+[H^3(H_{\bar z\bar z\bar z}+\epsilon)/3]_{\bar z}=0$. The leading matching conditions are $H\sim\bar z^2/\sqrt2$ as $\bar z\to-\infty$ and a bounded film, with vanishing derivatives, as $\bar z\to+\infty$. They connect the static bath-side <meniscus> to a uniform entrained film.

For positive wall speed varying on the outer time scale, neglect the order-$\epsilon$ terms at leading order and set
$$
H=v_0^{2/3}g(\zeta),\qquad\zeta=\bar z/v_0^{1/3}.
$$
The parabolic matching coefficient is unchanged because $v_0^{2/3}/v_0^{2/3}=1$. Both remaining differential terms scale as $v_0^{4/3}$, reducing the equation to $3g_\zeta+(g^3g_{\zeta\zeta\zeta})_\zeta=0$. The supplied universal solution therefore yields the <quasisteady drag-out matching law>
$$
\boxed{H\longrightarrow C_0v_0^{2/3},\qquad C_0=0.948,\quad\bar z\to+\infty.}
$$
Above the turn-around region, $H$ and its outer derivatives are order one, while $\delta^3\widehat C=\epsilon^3$ and $\delta^2\widehat C=\epsilon$. Hence the leading outer equation and inflow condition are
$$
\boxed{H_t+v_0(t)H_z=0,\qquad H(z_0^+,t)=C_0[v_0(t)]^{2/3}.}
$$
The boundary value comes from matching, not from setting the film to zero at the wall contact point.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Along a <characteristic>, $\dot z=(1+t)^{-2}$ and $\dot H=0$. Integration gives $z+1/(1+t)=\xi$, so $\boxed{H=F(\xi)}$. A <characteristic> entering through $z=z_0$ at time $\tau$ has $\xi=z_0+1/(1+\tau)$ and carries $H=C_0(1+\tau)^{-4/3}$. Thus the boundary-fed and initially dry portions give
$$
\boxed{F(\xi)=\begin{cases}C_0(\xi-z_0)^{4/3},&z_0<\xi\leq z_0+1,\\0,&\xi>z_0+1.\end{cases}}
$$
Values at $\xi\leq z_0$ are never sampled in the finite-time physical region and need not be prescribed. The moving coating front is $z=z_0+t/(1+t)$. Explicitly,
$$
\boxed{H(z,t)=\begin{cases}C_0\left(z-z_0+\dfrac1{1+t}\right)^{4/3},&0\leq z-z_0\leq\dfrac{t}{1+t},\\0,&z-z_0>\dfrac{t}{1+t}.\end{cases}}
$$
The incompatible initial corner creates a jump at the leading coating front; a trace convention at the jump does not affect the transported volume. The wall's total stroke is $\int_0^\infty v_0\,dt=1$, so within this leading transport model the final profile is
$$
\boxed{H_\infty(z)=\begin{cases}C_0(z-z_0)^{4/3},&0<z-z_0<1,\\0,&z-z_0>1.\end{cases}}
$$
Earlier-deposited fluid is thicker and sits farther up the finite coated interval. Its total scaled volume is $3C_0/7$, agreeing with $\int_0^\infty v_0H(z_0,t)\,dt=C_0\int_0^\infty(1+t)^{-10/3}dt$. This is a <finite-stroke coating profile> for the leading matched model.

